 rough.**.\n   - This is given directly.\n\n10. **Harry is smart.**.\n    - This is given directly.\n\n11. **All smart, round people are blue.**.\n    - Erin is smart and round, but Erin is not blue. This contradicts the given information, so this statement is false.\n\n12. **If Fiona is round, then Fiona is red.**.\n    - Fiona is round, so by the given statement, Fiona is red. This is consistent with the given information.\n\n13. **If someone is red, then they are young.**.\n    - Harry is red, so Harry is young. This is consistent with the given information.\n\n14. **If Harry is young and Harry is round, then Harry is rough.**.\n    - Harry is young and round, so by the given statement, Harry is rough. This is consistent with the given information.\n\n15. **If someone is young and smart, then they are blue.**.\n    - Harry is young and smart, so Harry should be blue. However, Harry is not blue. This contradicts the given information, so this statement is false.\n\n16. **White people are smart.**.\n    - Erin is white and smart, which is consistent with the given information.\n\n17. **Young people are round.**.\n    - Harry is young and round, which is consistent with the given information.\n\n18. **If someone is young, then they are white.**.\n    - Harry is young, so Harry is white. This is consistent with the given information.\n\n### Conclusion:.\n- Dave is blue.\n- Erin is red.\n- Fiona is red (from statement 12).\n- Harry is red (from statement 8 and 13).\n\nGiven the information, Dave is blue, and Erin, Fiona, and Harry are red. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
